;; detect-video.ab3 -- tells the launcher script which video standard this machine has, and whether
;; the machine can run the game at all.
;; A standalone program, not part of the game: compiled by compile-sparky-bin to
;; compiled/DetectVideo and run by the launcher script (S/SparkyDave) before the game, every start.
;; It has no OPTIMIZE line, so it is plain 68000 code and runs on any Amiga: the game is 68020 code
;; (OPTIMIZE 1) and on an A500 or A600 would crash before its own check.
;;
;; The machine check is the game's (MachineProblem{}, includes/sparkydave.machine.ab3), with the room
;; the game's code will need (#MACHINE_CODE_BYTES), as the game is not loaded yet. When the machine
;; lacks something, the message goes into the flag file RAM:sparky-machine-problem, which the script
;; types, waits and stops on; on a machine that can run it the file is deleted.
;;
;; It creates the flag file RAM:sparky-video-pal on a PAL machine and deletes it on an NTSC one,
;; and the script reads it with IF EXISTS. A return code does not work: proofs/video-standard.ab3
;; (run 2026-10-13) showed "End d0" never reaches the script's IF WARN, while the flag file
;; read right under both the PAL and the NTSC profile.

WBStartup

XINCLUDE "/includes/sparkydave.machine.ab3"

machineProblemFound.w = MachineProblem{#MACHINE_CODE_BYTES}
If machineProblemFound = #MACHINE_OK
  If Exists("RAM:sparky-machine-problem") > 0 Then KillFile "RAM:sparky-machine-problem"
Else
  If WriteFile(1, "RAM:sparky-machine-problem")
    FileOutput 1
    NPrint MachineProblemText{machineProblemFound, #MACHINE_CODE_BYTES}
    CloseFile 1
  EndIf
EndIf

If NTSC = True
  If Exists("RAM:sparky-video-pal") > 0 Then KillFile "RAM:sparky-video-pal"
Else
  If WriteFile(0, "RAM:sparky-video-pal")
    FileOutput 0
    NPrint "pal"
    CloseFile 0
  EndIf
EndIf

End
